{"participant_code": "Conversational_B_P6", "condition_label": "Conversational (B)", "task_name": "task_3", "task_label": "Task 3", "valid_selection": true, "n_violations": 0, "selected_quality": 0.907563025210084, "selected_review_budget": 0.34, "selected_training_fraction": 0.9, "selected_corruption_level": "none", "quality_change_from_base": 0.05772302521008399, "review_budget_reduction_pct": 9.333333333333327, "comment_text": "I prefer Option A because it has the highest quality, which is slightly better than Options B and C, even though the difference is small. The corruption level is the same across all options, while Option A has a lower review budget, which I prefer, even if it requires slightly more training data (0.90 vs 0.86).", "justification_words": 56}
{"participant_code": "Conversational_B_P7", "condition_label": "Conversational (B)", "task_name": "task_1", "task_label": "Task 1", "valid_selection": true, "n_violations": 0, "selected_quality": 0.8594249201277955, "selected_review_budget": 0.375, "selected_training_fraction": 0.55, "selected_corruption_level": "none", "quality_change_from_base": NaN, "review_budget_reduction_pct": NaN, "comment_text": "Option A has the highest F1 score (on par with B), with a lower review budget (less manual input required) and a lower Training Fraction (easier on computatinal requirements). It is unequivocally the best choice. One remark: B is listed as \"balanced compromise\" when it shows a similar F1 result with worse parameters than A (higher review Budget, higher training fraction). It feels like more like a direct downgrade to A than a compromise.", "justification_words": 74}
{"participant_code": "Conversational_B_P7", "condition_label": "Conversational (B)", "task_name": "task_2", "task_label": "Task 2", "valid_selection": true, "n_violations": 0, "selected_quality": 0.8239845261121856, "selected_review_budget": 0.25, "selected_training_fraction": 0.32, "selected_corruption_level": "none", "quality_change_from_base": NaN, "review_budget_reduction_pct": NaN, "comment_text": "Option A performs the best for all metrics except corruption level. B offers better robustness to corrupted data, but the trade-off seems too steep.", "justification_words": 24}
{"participant_code": "Conversational_B_P7", "condition_label": "Conversational (B)", "task_name": "task_3", "task_label": "Task 3", "valid_selection": true, "n_violations": 0, "selected_quality": 0.907563025210084, "selected_review_budget": 0.34, "selected_training_fraction": 0.9, "selected_corruption_level": "none", "quality_change_from_base": 0.05772302521008399, "review_budget_reduction_pct": 9.333333333333327, "comment_text": "I was easily able to gauge what intervals I should ask for by adjusting the metrics and looking at the alternatives table. F1 score between 0.85 and .95 was where I started getting diminishing returns and the marginal improvements started slowing down. For this reason the demaracation between A and B in terms of who is better seems less straightforward than before (probably because we're pushing the parameters to the limit) One remark: at F1 between 0.85 and 0.90 all three alternatives were exact copies of each other.", "justification_words": 88}
